VIM(1)                       Funny CLI Manual                       VIM(1)

NAME
    vim — Open a file in the terminal editor using a Vim-style
          command name, without actually launching Vim.

SYNOPSIS
    vim <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'vim' command is an alternate name for the CLI's default
    'edit' command.

    Despite its name, this command does NOT invoke the actual Vim
    editor.

    Instead, it passes all arguments directly to 'edit', which
    creates and runs the application's TerminalEditor.

    In practical terms:

        vim <file>

    is effectively:

        edit <file>

    'edit' remains the canonical command. 'vim' simply exists for
    users whose muscle memory prefers typing 'vim'.

EDITOR SELECTION
    edit
        The default and recommended command for the built-in editor.

    nano
        Familiar Unix-style name for the same TerminalEditor.
        Does NOT launch GNU nano.

    vim
        Vim-style command name for the same TerminalEditor.
        Does NOT launch Vim.

    vi
        Classic vi-style command name for the same TerminalEditor.
        Does NOT launch vi.

    The command names differ.
    The editor does not.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The file to open in the terminal editor.

        Required by the underlying 'edit' command.

BEHAVIOR
    All arguments received by 'vim' are passed unchanged to:

        self.cmd_edit(args)

    Therefore, path handling, editor behavior, and error reporting
    are all inherited from 'edit'.

    It is 'edit' with a Vim-shaped name.

EXAMPLES
    > edit config.py
        The canonical way to open config.py.

    > vim config.py
        Opens config.py in the same TerminalEditor.

        You typed 'vim'.
        No Vim was harmed in the execution of this command.

    > vim README.md
        Opens README.md using the default editor implementation.

FUN FACTS
    - 'edit' is the canonical editor command.
    - 'vim' is only an alternate command name.
    - Actual Vim is not required.
    - No Vim configuration files are loaded by this command.
    - No Vim installation is involved.
    - File completion is enabled.
    - The command delegates directly to 'edit'.
    - The name says Vim. The implementation says "surprise."

